1. Security as a Distributed "Immunology"
Instead of a single, massive military force patrolling the world, your Archipelago model treats security like an immune system.

Drone Surveillance as "Sensory Nodes": Rather than weapons-heavy drones, you are describing a high-definition, constant-monitoring network. This network wouldn't be "owned" by a nation, but would be an Open-Source Security Utility managed by the global protocol.

The "Vigilance API": If a sensor detects sabotage on a pipeline or an unauthorized attempt to breach a node, the system doesn't just alert a government; it broadcasts the Provenance of the Attack to the entire network. This makes "clandestine sabotage" nearly impossible, as the identity and origin of the threat are captured in real-time.

2. Preventing "Centralized Vigilance" (The Director Trap)
The risk of "Strict International Government Vigilance" is that it creates a new type of "Director" who controls all the drones. To keep this from happening, the Governance of Vigilance must be as decentralized as the energy grid:

Shared Authority: No single nation or leader has the "kill-switch" for the drone network. Access to the sensor data is governed by the same Multi-Party Consensus that manages the energy and research pools.

Algorithmic Accountability: The drones and monitoring systems operate on code that is public and immutable. They cannot be programmed to target specific people or dissenters; they are programmed only to detect Protocol Breaches (e.g., sabotage, illegal resource hoarding, or unverified research).

3. The "Uninhabited Island" Threat
You raised a crucial point: as we use these islands to build the future, they could be targeted by actors who remain outside the protocol or operate from the shadows.

The "Protocol Defense": Because these islands are interconnected nodes, they are not isolated. If one node is attacked, the others are instantly alerted. The "Vigilance Protocol" acts as a tripwire.

Passive Hardening: By design, these artificial islands would be built with automated, self-repairing infrastructure. If an element tries to sabotage a section, the system automatically detects the change in pressure or data flow and isolates that section, preventing the "cascading failure" that is so common in today’s centralized grids.

